{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` is at **800 lines against the hard 800-line cap**\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which the CI module-size gate enforces with no allowlist. It\nis one line from red: the next change to any public library write verb fails the build. The\nsibling story that adds two optional `create_ticket` arguments has to widen two `@overload`\nsignatures, the implementation, and their docstrings — it cannot land at all until this is\nrelieved. This ticket buys that headroom by concern, not by line count.\n\n## Approach\n\n`_lib_writes` is the **aggregation point for every public library write**, so it grows on two\nindependent axes: a new verb appends a wrapper, and an existing verb gaining an argument widens\none in place. The 2015 facade split (S3/4532) cut the library **by direction** — writes vs reads\nvs gates vs ops — and that single axis is exhausted: the write half alone now carries seven\nconcerns. The durable fix is to cut the write half **by concern**, following the `fsck` split\n(`defaceable-icy-antelope`), which took two cuts out of one 800-line file.\n\nThe seven concerns present today:\n\n1. **Genesis** — `init_repo`, `create_ticket` (two `@overload`s + impl), `idea`.\n2. **Identity & key material** — `create_identity`, `ensure_identity_for`, `create_placeholder`,\n   `add_identity_key`, `revoke_identity_key`, `use_identity`, `resolve_current_identity`. All\n   delegate to `rebar._commands.identity`; none touches ticket state.\n3. **Status lifecycle** — `transition`, `claim`, `reopen`. The only place in the module that\n   raises `ConcurrencyError`.\n4. **Leaf mutations** — `_python_leaf` and its seven in-module callers `comment`, `edit_ticket`,\n   `link`, `unlink`, `tag`, `untag`, `archive`.\n5. **Session logs** — `append_session_log`, `start_session_log` (own `CommandError` mapping;\n   they do NOT route through `_python_leaf`).\n6. **Store maintenance** — `compact`, `attach_commits`.\n7. **Signing** — `sign_manifest`, `verify_signature`, delegating to `rebar.signing`.\n\n**Two cuts**, mirroring `fsck`:\n\n- **`_lib_mutations.py`** takes concerns 4+5+6. Anchored on the `_python_leaf` call graph: one\n  adapter with seven callers, all inside the block, and nothing outside it in this module calls\n  it. Stated honestly, 5 and 6 **ride along** because they are the contiguous remainder of the\n  post-genesis mutation surface, not because they call `_python_leaf`; the strict call-graph\n  subset is ~130 lines and non-contiguous, which would force a scattered cut close to the\n  100-line floor.\n- **`_lib_identity.py`** takes concerns 2+7 — the non-ticket, key-material surface. Identities\n  own OpenSSH key lines, `use_identity`/`resolve_current_identity` select the current one, and\n  signing binds a manifest to the environment key. Stated honestly, signing is ~52 lines and\n  **cannot stand alone** under the 100-line floor; identity is the nearest concern by shape and\n  the module already banners signing apart from the write path.\n- **`_lib_writes.py` keeps concerns 1+3** — genesis and the status state machine, which is what\n  the module's name should mean.\n\nThe plan of record extracted only concerns 4+5+6 and left `_lib_writes` at ~544. That was sound\nwhen the file was 778; at 800 it lands **above** the 200-500 target band on a file that grew 22\nlines in weeks, so the single cut is re-baselined into two.\n\nMoved names are **re-exported** from `_lib_writes` with `# noqa: F401`, exactly as `fsck.py`\nre-exports from `fsck_scan`/`fsck_tracker_health`. That leaves `__init__.py` (which re-exports\n24 names) and `_lib_gates.py` (which imports `_python_leaf`) **untouched**, so every\n`rebar.<name>` and `rebar._lib_writes.<name>` path survives with a smaller diff than\nre-pointing consumers. Dependency direction is one-way — `_lib_writes` imports the two new\nmodules, neither imports back — so there is no cycle.\n\nThis is a **behaviour-preserving move**: function bodies are relocated byte-identically and no\nlogic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py` (new) — concerns 4+5+6\n- `src/rebar/_lib_identity.py` (new) — concerns 2+7\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — keeps concerns 1+3, gains the two re-export blocks\n\n`__init__.py`, `_lib_gates.py`, and every file under `tests/` are deliberately **not** touched.\nThe reference audit supports that: the only real-code importers of `_lib_writes` are\n`_lib_gates.py` (`_python_leaf`) and `__init__.py` (the 24-name re-export), and the string pass\n(`monkeypatch.setattr` / `getattr` / `importlib`) finds **zero** references to `_lib_writes`\nattributes anywhere — every test patch targets the `rebar` facade or `rebar.signing`, both\npreserved. `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py` references the **path**\n`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py`, which does not move.\n\n`.github/complexity-baseline.json` holds **zero** entries for `_lib_writes.py`, so no moved\nsymbol carries accepted debt and the `--lock` re-key hazard that cast `Verified -1` on the\n`fsck` change cannot arise. The baseline file is therefore not in scope.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the oracle is the existing suite passing with **no test file edited**. The\nspecific risk is the **public API surface**, since 24 names are re-exported as `rebar.<name>`\nand `_lib_gates` depends on `_python_leaf` at three call sites — a move that silently dropped a\nre-export would leave the suite green in files that import the facade lazily. That is checked\nby name rather than assumed:\n\n- every name previously importable as `rebar.<name>` still resolves after the move, asserted\n  name by name against the pre-move list;\n- `rebar._lib_writes.<name>` still resolves for all moved names, which is what makes\n  `_lib_gates` and any `monkeypatch` target survive;\n- the three modules import cleanly in **either** order, catching a cycle introduced by the\n  re-export direction.\n\nThe existing `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` is the size oracle and needs no edit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_lib_writes.py` is at or below 450 lines; checked by `wc -l` on the landed file and by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passing.\n- [ ] `_lib_mutations.py` and `_lib_identity.py` are each between 100 and 500 lines inclusive;\n      checked by `wc -l` on both landed files.\n- [ ] Every name previously importable as `rebar.<name>` from `_lib_writes` — all 24, including\n      `_python_leaf` — still resolves; checked by importing `rebar` and asserting `hasattr` for\n      each name in the pre-move list.\n- [ ] Every moved name still resolves as `rebar._lib_writes.<attr>`; checked by importing\n      `rebar._lib_writes` and asserting `hasattr` for each moved name.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/__init__.py`, `src/rebar/_lib_gates.py`, and every file under `tests/` are\n      unchanged; checked by confirming the landed commit's file list is exactly the three files\n      in Scope.\n- [ ] The three modules import cleanly in any order; checked by running a fresh interpreter that\n      imports `rebar._lib_identity`, `rebar._lib_mutations`, `rebar._lib_writes` in that order\n      and again in reverse.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` are green; checked by running both.\n- [ ] The CI test selection passes with no test-file edit; checked by running\n      `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` and confirming `git diff --stat` shows no\n      path under `tests/`.\n- [ ] `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --lock` reports no new entries; checked by running\n      it with `GITHUB_REPOSITORY=navapbc/rebar`.","title":"Split _lib_writes by concern into mutations and identity"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786606902783707001,"uuid":"d766521e-bd9a-435e-b622-99828bf50de9"}